The trigonometric ratios of the angle A in right triangle ABC (see Fig. 8.4) are defined as follows :

\text {sine of} \angle \mathrm{A} = \frac {\text {side opposite to angle A}}{\text {hypotenuse}} = \frac {\mathrm{BC}}{\mathrm{AC}}

\text {cosine of} \angle \mathrm{A} = \frac {\text {side adjacent to angle A}}{\text {hypotenuse}} = \frac {\mathrm{AB}}{\mathrm{AC}}

\text {tangent of} \angle \mathrm{A} = \frac {\text {side opposite to angle A}}{\text {side adjacent to angle A}} = \frac {\mathrm{BC}}{\mathrm{AB}}

\angle \mathrm{A} = \frac {1}{\text {sine of} \angle \mathrm{A}} = \frac {\text {hypotenuse}}{\text {side opposite to angle A}} = \frac {\mathrm{AC}}{\mathrm{BC}}

secant of $\angle A = \frac{1}{\cosine of \angle A} = \frac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{side adjacent to angle A}} = \frac{AC}{AB}$

cotangent of $\angle A = \frac{1}{\text{tangent of} \angle A} = \frac{\text{side adjacent to angle A}}{\text{side opposite to angle A}} = \frac{AB}{BC}$

The ratios defined above are abbreviated as sin A, cos A, tan A, cosec A, sec A and cot A respectively. Note that the ratios cosec A, sec A and cot A are respectively, the reciprocals of the ratios sin A, cos A and tan A.

Also, observe that $\tan A = \frac{BC}{AB} = \frac{\frac{BC}{AC}}{\frac{AB}{AC}} = \frac{\sin A}{\cos A}$ and $\cot A = \frac{\cos A}{\sin A}$.

So, the trigonometric ratios of an acute angle in a right triangle express the relationship between the angle and the length of its sides.

